WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
Hey there, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 10 for March 22nd, 2026.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.740
Some awesome AI developments today and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.920
Let's get into it. Imagine a student who doesn't just do their math homework.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.660
This student also invents better ways to study,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.960
tests those new methods on themselves, and actually gets smarter faster.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.440
That is basically what just happened with a new model from a Chinese company called Minimax.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.840
They released a model named M2.7 that reportedly took an active role in improving its own training.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.100
Instead of humans doing all the work, the model used what are called autonomous optimization loops.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.980
Those are basically self-running experiments where the model tries different ways to learn,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.140
sees what works best, and adjusts its own training approach.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.000
And amazingly, it's still achieved competitive results on tests.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.060
Think of it like this. Normally building an AI is like a coach designing every single drill for an

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.160
athlete. Here, the athlete started suggesting better drills and testing them too.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.380
This matters because most AI models are built entirely by teams of engineers who decide exactly how

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:10.000
to train them. If models can start helping design their own learning process, it could speed up how fast

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.380
new AI's improve. It might even make them better at complex tasks that need creative problem-solving.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.440
For students and creators, this points toward future tools that understand their own weaknesses and

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:25.200
fix them, kind of like a super smart homework helper that knows when it's guessing and tells you.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.800
The big so-what for you is that AI is moving from something humans fully control to systems that

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.940
can participate in their own growth. That raises cool questions about how much we should trust AI to

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:44.400
improve itself and what kinds of safeguards we need. You cannot try M2.7 directly today, but you can

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.320
experiment with the idea of self-checking AI right now. Open chat GPT or Google Gemini both free

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:57.120
and try this. Ask it a question you know the answer to. Then say, now rate your confidence in that

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.240
answer from one to 10 and explain why. Watch how it evaluates itself. That is the beginning of the

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.660
uncertainty aware systems experts are building. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, let's do a

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:13.940
proper deep dive into how safe testing strategies for AI models actually work. You know how when you want

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:19.580
to try a new hairstyle, you do not just chop off all your hair at once. You might test it on a small

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.700
section first, ask a couple friends what they think or use an app to see a photo of the change before

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:30.860
committing. That same careful approach is exactly how smart companies test new AI models before showing

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:36.480
them to millions of users. Imagine your school has two lunch menus, the normal one everyone knows,

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.120
and a brand new experimental one. Instead of forcing everyone to eat the new food, they could try

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.860
four safe methods. First, they could randomly give half the students the new menu and half the old

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.860
one. This is called a B testing and they see who is happier. Second, they could test the new menu only

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.720
with one small group like just the year 9s before rolling it out to the whole school. This is called

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.640
canary testing, like sending a canary in first to check if it is safe. Third, they could mix both menus

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.840
together so every student gets some old and some new items in random order. This is interleave testing

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.080
and they compare reactions bite by bite. Finally, they could secretly prepare the new menu in the

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:18.100
kitchen but keep serving the old one while secretly measuring how the new recipes would have performed.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:23.200
This is shadow testing. The real menu never changes until they are sure the new one is better

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.700
and that is basically what these four controlled strategies are doing with AI. Companies run the new

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:34.460
model alongside the old one in careful ways so they can catch problems before anyone gets a bad

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:39.840
experience. Next time you hear about deploying machine learning models to production, you can tell

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.180
your friends it is basically the same caution you would use before changing your whole hairstyle

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:51.400
and that is basically how safe testing strategies for AI models work. Not so scary, right? All right,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:57.240
let us talk about some cool stuff you can actually try today. A new survey found that 95% of British

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:03.420
students are using generative AI. Generative AI just means systems that create new content like text

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.800
or pictures instead of only searching for existing stuff. But their feelings are totally split. Some

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.560
say it helps them understand topics better and makes learning more interesting. Others worry it is

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.920
quietly replacing their ability to think for themselves. Universities are struggling to keep up with these

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:25.040
changes. This is super relevant if you are in school or college right now. AI is not some far off thing.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.760
It is already part of how most students work. The survey shows both the excitement and the honest

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:36.680
concerns which is a great reminder that you get to decide how you use these tools. Try this today.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:43.740
Open a free AI-like chat GPT Claude or Gemini. Pick something you are studying this week, maybe a

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:50.140
history topic or science concept. First, write your own one paragraph explanation, then ask the AI

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:55.440
to explain the same thing. Compare the two. Ask yourself, did the AI help me understand it better

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.580
or did I just copy? This exact experiment is what thousands of students are figuring out right now.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.740
Here is another interesting one. A big publisher called Heshette Book Group decided not to publish a

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.600
horror novel called Shy Girl. They were worried the text was generated by artificial intelligence.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.120
This shows how seriously some big publishers are taking the question of AI created books. It

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:21.640
matters because a lot of people care whether a story was written by a human or by AI. For anyone who

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:27.040
loves reading, writing stories or dreams of publishing one day, this news highlights the ongoing

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.840
conversation about creativity and AI. You can try something related right now. Go to any free AI

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:38.760
writing tool like chat GPT. Ask it to write the first page of a horror story about a shy girl who

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:44.040
discovers something strange in her mirror. Then write your own version of the same scene. Compare them.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.260
Notice what feels different. This simple side-by-side test helps you see what the conversation is really

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:56.380
about. Time for a few quick bits to finish us off. Open AI is reportedly growing from about 4,500

